<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qilian Mountains</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Qilian Mountains are a range on the north-eastern edge of the Tibetan Plateau, along the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> and Qinghai in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Their snowmelt waters the oases of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a>, the route of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> between China and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. Chinese sources place the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> between the Qilian Mountains and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qilian-mountains.jpg" alt="Qilian Mountains" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Neil Young (cnneil (Flickr) · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qilian_in_Qilian_Qinghai.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Qilian Mountains are a range on the north-eastern edge of the Tibetan Plateau, forming the border between the Chinese provinces of Qinghai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>. Together with the Altyn-Tagh they are sometimes called the Nan Shan, since they lie south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a>, and they are northern outliers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunlun-mountains">Kunlun Mountains</a>. The range runs about 800 km south-east from south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> along the south-western side of the Hexi Corridor.</p><p>The peak called Qilian Shan, about 60 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiuquan">Jiuquan</a>, rises to 5,547 m and is the highest of the main range, though two peaks farther south are higher: Kangze'gyai at 5,808 m and Qaidam Shan at 5,759 m. In the east the range passes north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinghai-lake">Qinghai Lake</a> and ends near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanzhou">Lanzhou</a>, and sections of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a> run along its northern slopes.</p><p>Many mostly small rivers rise in the range and flow north-east, supporting irrigated farming in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a> before they vanish in the Alashan Desert. The best known is the Ejin, or Heihe. The range holds many glaciers, the largest being the Touming Mengke, and the large brackish Lake Hala. Biandukou, a pass above 3,500 m, links Minle County in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> with Qilian County in Qinghai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a> mentions the Qilian Mountains together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> as the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a>. It has been suggested, however, that these were the mountains now called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a>, 1,500 km to the west. The Tang commentator Yan Shigu said that Qilian was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> word meaning sky. Sanping Chen proposed that it shared an origin with the Chinese words for heaven, while Schuessler objects that it was not Xiongnu and derives it from an old word for heaven that is also linked to Tibetan. The Tuyuhun were based around the range, which was formerly known in European languages as the Richthofen Range after the explorer and geologist Ferdinand von Richthofen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qilian Mountains” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to the Shiji of Sima Qian, which draws on the report of the envoy Zhang Qian, they originally lived between Dunhuang and the <b>Qilian Mountains</b> in the Gansu corridor, and in the late third century BCE they were strong enough to look down on the Xiongnu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
